<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Britain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was the air campaign fought over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the summer and autumn of 1940, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> defended the United Kingdom against the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s failure to win air superiority led Hitler to postpone the planned invasion of Britain indefinitely, the first major German defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain.jpg" alt="Battle of Britain" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Australian armed forces · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spitfire_and_He_111_during_Battle_of_Britain_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had rapidly overwhelmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to face the threat of invasion by sea. The German high command recognised the difficulty of a seaborne attack while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, and its primary objective was to compel Britain to accept a negotiated peace. On 16 July 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> ordered preparation of Operation Sea Lion, an amphibious and airborne assault to follow once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> had air superiority over the Channel. Britain officially dates the battle from 10 July to 31 October 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> first attacked coastal convoys and ports. On 1 August it was directed to achieve air superiority and incapacitate RAF Fighter Command, and twelve days later it shifted its attacks to airfields and infrastructure. The RAF's Hurricanes and Spitfires faced the Bf 109, and Fighter Command relied on the Chain Home radar and a system of reporting chains named for Hugh Dowding. About 20 per cent of the pilots were from outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, among them 145 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, 127 New Zealanders and 112 Canadians.</p><p>On 7 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> began the night attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and other cities known as the Blitz. At a conference on 14 September Hitler acknowledged that it had not gained the air superiority that Sea Lion required. On 15 September the Luftwaffe made its largest bombing attack yet, which forced the whole of the RAF to defend London and the south-east and ended in a decisive British victory. In September RAF Bomber Command night raids also disrupted the German preparation of converted barges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 19 September Hitler ordered a reduction in work on Sea Lion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> proved unable to sustain daylight raids, though its night bombing continued until 11 May 1941. By 2 November the RAF fielded 1,796 pilots, over 40 per cent more than in July. The battle was the first major military campaign fought entirely by air forces and the first major defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s military forces. Churchill summed it up with the words, Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few, and the pilots have been known as The Few ever since.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Britain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fast campaigns combining armoured divisions and air power overran Poland, Denmark, Norway, the Low Countries and France by June 1940, but the Luftwaffe lost the <b>Battle of Britain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…War the United Kingdom declared war on Germany on 3 September 1939, evacuated over 330,000 Allied troops from Dunkirk in 1940, held off the Luftwaffe in the <b>Battle of Britain</b> and, under Winston Churchill, fought on until joined by the Soviet Union and the United States in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The test led to the Chain Home network, vital in the <b>Battle of Britain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Luftwaffe began attacking Channel convoys and coastal targets, the date Britain uses for the start of the <b>Battle of Britain</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
